
  <sn><md>### CLASSIFICATION OF CARBOHYDRATES

| Feature | Saccharides (Sugars) | Polysaccharides (Complex sugars) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Physical Properties** | • Low molecular weight<br>• Soluble in water<br>• Sweet to taste | • High molecular weight<br>• Insoluble in water<br>• Tasteless |
| **Composition** | **Monosaccharides:** Simple sugars<br>**Disaccharides:** Double sugars | **Multiple sugars** |
| **Diagrammatic Representation** | [Monosaccharide/Disaccharide representation] | [Polysaccharide representation] |
| **General Formula** | $(CH_2O)_n$ (when $n = 3$ to $7$) | $C_x(H_2O)_n$ |
| **Common Examples** | • Glyceraldehyde, Glucose, Fructose, Galactose, Ribose sugar *(Monosaccharides)*<br>• Maltose, Sucrose, Lactose *(Disaccharides)* | Starch, Glycogen, Cellulose, Lignin, Chitin |

*Note on Disaccharides Formula:* $C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}$
*Note on Structure:* The bond linking disaccharides/polysaccharides together is called a `Glycosidic bond`.

</md></sn>
